    "설명글(영)": 1,
    "Its unique geography, with the Nakdong river flowing around the village making a large semi circle, this village is named Hahoe, meaning winding river. Also, the shape resembles a lotus flower on the water. Lotus flowers make the fruit and flower together, so it symbolizes a good harvest and affluence at the same time. It might be a good reason for the prosperity of Hahoe village from generation to generation. It also is thought to resemble a ship. Unlike other folk villages, Hahoe Village is still populated by one main family. 70 percent of the people who live here have the same family name,\"Pungsan Ryu\",a family who has lived here for 600 years. To this day, Andong maintains a large number of clan villages inhabited by same-name family members. Hahoe Village received international attention when Queen Elizabeth Ⅱ visited in 1999. \n\nBefore the Ryu clan came, the Heo clan had settled here, followed by the An clan. Finally, the Ryu clan settled here, and prospered enough to maintain permanent control of the village. Still, within the village, each location was different. Ryu Jonghye decided to live in Hahoe Village after he learned that Hahoe was a good place to live, surrounded by scenery such as mountains, a sandy beach, and Mansongjeong pine forest. From early to late Joseon Dynasty, the houses have been well-preserved. Various sizes of houses mingle at Hahoe village- from small workers' houses to large residences, such as the aristocrats' houses - Yangjindang house, Chunghyodang house, Bukchoondae house. Hence Hahoe is the place representing the lifestyle of Korean ancestors. \nThe entire village of Hahoe has been designated an Important Folk Material and a protected area since 1984.": 1,
    "Byeongsan Seowon is located in the nature, with buildings like a part of the scenery arount it and this makes Byeongsan Seowon one of beautiful traditional buildings in Korea. The name of Byeongsan Seowon was from the fact a mountain across Nakdong-gang(a river) from Seowon looks like Byeongpung(a traditional partition). \n\nBSSW is included in 5 Seowon in Joseon Dynasty, with Dodong Seowon, Dosan Seowon, Sosu Seowon, and Oksan Seowon. In accordance with the principle of “a school at front, a tomb at back”, the front area of Seowon was built for a school and the back area where was the highest location was used for a shrine. \n\nBSSW was built in the mid-Joseon Dynasty, when the spirits of Seowon was the highest. It inherited the traditions of early Seowon, such as Sosu Seowon and Dosan Seowon very well and showed the soundness of Confucian studies before the studies were gone for theories and formalities as the model Seowon. \n\nParticularly, understanding of the location and relationships among the buildings shows the flexibility of ancestors. Traditional geomantic principles and Confucian ideologies which endeavors for the Utopia of respects were well-compromised.": 1,
    "Dosanseowon, located in Togye-ri, Dosan-myeon, Andong-si, Gyoungbuk, was built to admire and memory the scholarship and virtue of Toegye Leehwang (Seonjo 7). Its buildings are decorated neatly and simply, which properly reflects his attitude and personality as a scholar. \n\nIn the structural viewpoint, Dosanseowon divided into Dosanseodang and Dosanseowon. Dosanseodang is the place he, by himself, taught his followers, whereas Dosanseodang was built after his death. \n\nDosanseodang was built in 1561 (Myeongjong 16) and the oldest builing in it. It is said to be designed by him and the purpose was to cultivate his followers and pursue studies after he retired into country. Nongunjeongsa used for a boarding house and Hagojiksa used for attached facility were built together simultaneously. \n\nDosanseowon was completed in 1576, which is 6 years after his death. In the year of 1570 when he passed away, it was decided to place his mortuary tablet in Sangdeoksa (Treasury No. 211). After 2 years, by suggestion of local Confucians a Sadang was constructed to protect the mortuary tablet, then Jeongyodang (Treasury No. 210) and Dong, Seojae was constructed to be completed as a whole building. As granted tablet written by Han-Seokbong, it, as a Saaekseowon, became the head place of Confucianism in Youngnam in 1575 (Seonjo 8). Sarim placed Sir. Wolcheon Jomok in the place in 1615 (Gwanghaegun 7). \n\nDosanseowon is consist of shrine space and attached building centering main educational facility on it. The whole educational facility is arranged with bisymmetry centering Jindomun which is a gate and Jeongyodang situated in the middle on it. Gwangmyeongsil divided into west and east is a stack room, which we can consider as a library of today. Dong, Seojae are facilities where Yusaengs dwell and study. We call the eastern building \"Bakyakjae\" and the western building \"Honguijae\", which are facing each other. Jeongyodang located in the center consists with a lecture room and a principal room, and Jangpangak keeping tablets is at the back of Dongjae. \n\nEnshrine space located on the highest point of dewon includes Jeonsacheong where prepare the various religious service and Danddeoksa where mortuary tablet is placed. Attached buildings include Sanggojiksa controlling and maintaining the Seowon, which is located at the back of Honguijae. \n\nOkjingak is at the left side of Seowon's gate where his mementos are displayed. \n\nCentering on this Seowon, 19 Pil (324.945m2) of woodland, paddies and dry fields were designated as a Historical place No. 170 to become sanctuary of Korean spiritual hometown of Confucianism in 1969. In 1977, Dosanseowon Control office was established and maintenance regulations were enacted and promulgated and up to now.": 1,
    "Bongjeongsa Temple (Taejang-ri, Seohu-myeon, Andong city) \nDuring the twelfth year of King Munmu's reign during the Silla Dynasty (672), Monk Euisang flew a paper phoenix into the sky from Buseoksa Temple. It has been said that the temple was constructed on the site where this paper phoenix was placed, and thus called 'Bongjeong Temple.'('Bong' means phoenix, while 'Jeong' menas stay) It contains many cultural artifacts, such as Geuk'rakjeon, the oldest wooden building in Korea. Daeungjeon, Hwawomkangdang and Gokumdang were constructed during the Joseon Dynasty (1392-1910), which contain valuable materials for architectural research. Additionally, there are three-story pagodas called Deokhuiru, Yosachae, and several small Buddhist temples, such as Yeongsanam hall and Jijoam hall.": 1,
    "Andong Folk Museum \nAndong Folk Museum is a indoor folk museum located in Andong \ndam. It displays over 7,000 Andong's unique tradition and culture \nincluding various artifacts. \n\nWolyeong Bridge \nThe largest wooden bridge in korea with 3.6m wide and 387m \nlong. Wolyeong-gyo is in harmony with moonlight on the lake \nand mountains surrounding the Nakdong River, which remain \nus of a part of an oriental painting. Nearby are the Palgakjeong \nPavillion and fountains, all of which add another beauty \nto the area.": 1,
    "An Innovative Museum Born of Andong's Tradition and Cutting Edge Digital Technology \nThis innovative museum allows visitors to fully experience Andong's Confucian culture, which became an important part of Korea's 5,000 year old history. These exhibits come to life, thanks to cutting edge technology.": 1,
    "This confucian shrine enshrines Kim seon-pteong, Kwon haeng and Jang jeong-pil, three meritorious retainers who performed distinguished services for founding the Goryeo dynasty. The shrine has 22pieces of relics including a treasure": 1,
    "Confucianism Land is a major facility within the Andong Cultural Tourism Complex which is a central recreational accommodation hub in the Northern Gyeongbuk culture of Confucianism. By setting a space for edutainment(education + entertainment that provides enthusiastic learning to all generations including kindergarteners, elementary school, middle school, and high school students, Confucianism Land enables the originally difficult Confucianism culture to be easily understood. It is an exhibition experience center where visitors can personally experience the life process of scholars in the 16th century Andong Daedong village through a time tunnel from the reality. In the circular 3D theater, which is the last course of the facility, viewing is even more entertaining due to dynamic 360 degrees circular walls that display special effects including 3D videos. I hope you will visit Confucianism Land which is to newly open, and personally experience the entertainment and educative contents regarding Confucianism.": 1,
    "This 387-meter bridge is the longest wooden pedestrian overpass in Korea. The name of Woryeonggyo Bridge was selected out of the 332 names submitted by locals. It was inspired by Woryeongdae Pavilion, Wolgok-myeon, and Eumdalgol Village, which were relocated due to the construction of a dam. Woryeongjeong Pavilion is located in the center of the bridge, offering visitors a place to relax.": 1
